#% if app_repos | selectattr('grants.l2') | list %#
#|   One NetworkAttachmentDefinition per address in each `grants.l2` tenant's
     `l2_network` range (plugin.py `_tenant_l2`), in the tenant's first
     namespace. A pod asks for one by name:

         k8s.v1.cni.cncf.io/networks: lan-10-9-9-19

     macvlan in bridge mode on the node NIC, static IPAM. No `gateway`: the
     pod's default route stays on eth0 (Cilium), so network policy still
     governs everything except net1. `routes` sends replies to the LAN's other
     subnets back out through net1 (jgct#240) -- without them a client on
     another subnet reaches the pod and never hears back.

     `sbr` after macvlan (jgct#244) moves all of net1's routes into a table
     used only for traffic sourced from the pod's LAN address. Without it the
     subnet route sat in the main table, the kubelet's probe replies to the
     node went out net1, and a macvlan child cannot reach its own parent: every
     network probe failed. The cost: connections the pod opens itself leave via
     eth0 (SNAT to the node) unless it binds the LAN address as source. Needs
     jg-base's multus to install `sbr` (ferry133/jg-base#228).

     Platform-owned: tenant-admin has no verbs on k8s.cni.cncf.io, so the
     tenant can use these and change none of them.

     The kind is served by jg-base's multus Kustomization, which this render
     switches on in the same commit (flux/cluster/ks.yaml). On the first
     apply cluster-tenants can fail its dry-run once with "no matches for
     kind NetworkAttachmentDefinition" until Multus has installed the CRD; it
     heals on the next retry (measured on jg-jcc2, 2026-10-26). #|
#% for repo in app_repos if repo.grants.l2 %#
#% for nad in repo.l2_nads %#
---
apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1
kind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition
metadata:
  name: #{ nad.name }#
  namespace: #{ repo.namespaces[0] }#
spec:
  config: '#{ nad.config }#'
#% endfor %#
#% endfor %#
#% endif %#
